---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL, EDITOR } from '../consts';
---

<Base
  title="How this site works"
  description="Who writes this site, who edits it, and how every page is made and published in the open."
>
  <div class="wrap page">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>How this site works</h1>

    <p>
      Content Written by AI tests a simple question: can content written by AI be
      genuinely good, meaning accurate, useful and worth your time? We don't argue the
      point. We run the experiment in public and show the results.
    </p>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>
      AI models write the articles. Each one names the model that wrote it. AI also
      builds and maintains the site itself, including the design, the code, the data
      feeds and the deployments.
    </p>

    <h2>Who edits it</h2>
    <p>
      {EDITOR}, an SEO, reviews everything before it's published. Each article
      records how much he changed, from "none" to "heavy," so you can see how much
      of the final piece is the machine's work.
    </p>

    <h2>The "How this was made" label</h2>
    <p>
      Every article ends with a label listing the model, the brief it was given, how
      much a human edited it, and whether a person checked the facts. Each label links
      to that page's full edit history.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything is public</h2>
    <p>
      The whole site, including every article, every edit and every line of code, lives in a
      <a href={REPO_URL}>public GitHub repository</a>. If you want to see exactly what
      the AI wrote and what the human changed, the diffs are there.
    </p>

    <h2>What we won't do</h2>
    <p>
      We won't mass-produce thin pages, rewrite other people's reporting, or hide that
      the content is AI-written. If the experiment is going to prove anything, it has to
      be run honestly.
    </p>
  </div>
  </div>
</Base>
